\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Comparison with the Nielsen–Ninomiya theorem}
\date{2026-11-23}

\p{Freudenthal subdivides a parallelepiped by the partial sums
of its edge vectors in every possible order
\citet{Section 1, pp. 580–581; Wintraecken translation, pp. 1–2}{freudenthal1942simplizial}.
Kuhn obtains the canonical subdivision of a cube by sorting its
coordinates \citet{p. 520, Lemma 1, its Corollary and Figure 3}{kuhn1960combinatorial}.
Gluing opposite faces of these subdivided grid cells gives the
periodic simplices used in the signed count of \ref{fdip-0140}.}

\p{The version of the Nielsen–Ninomiya theorem discussed in \ref{fdip-000T} applies to
free, phase-invariant, translation-invariant local Hamiltonians
on an infinite lattice in odd spatial dimension. The momentum
symbol is Hermitian on the continuous torus, the charge
projections are smooth, and the zeros are isolated with the
specified relativistic linear terms. Under these hypotheses the
total chiral index vanishes for each charge
\citet{Sections 1–2, pp. 481–484, Equations (6), (10)–(11)}{friedan1982nielsen}.
The finite balance in \ref{fdip-0140} uses oriented faces of
the sampled grid over an ordered field.}

\p{The derivative signs at the centered symbol's corners agree
with their finite degrees. The example with #{N=8} in
\ref{fdip-0142} shows that finite range alone does not
imply this agreement.}
